\ The five stones for each of the 46 visible drops.
\ This is the exact five-value recurrence in section E of the main rules.
\ All five new values use the same old row; none uses a partly new row.
\ The caller begins with drop 1 and advances through drops 2 to 46.
\ CAL compilation has not yet been verified.

A stone row has
  a wide quantity called wheat,
  a wide quantity called barley,
  a wide quantity called salt,
  a wide quantity called bitter,
  a wide quantity called red.

A stone formula has
  a wide quantity called base,
  a wide quantity called neighbor,
  a wide quantity called extra,
  a number called factor,
  a wide quantity called result.

The current stones are a stone row.
The next stones are a stone row.
The working stone formula is a stone formula.

\ Scratch values have unique declared CAL names, avoiding compound variable/type ambiguity.
The stonescale is a wide quantity.
The stonefactortext is a string.
The stonefactor is a wide quantity.
The stoneindex is a string.
The stoneposition is a number.

To begin the stone sequence:
  Put "17" into the current stones' wheat.
  Put "29" into the current stones' barley.
  Put "43" into the current stones' salt.
  Put "71" into the current stones' bitter.
  Put "101" into the current stones' red.

\ The recipe computes SAVE(base squared + factor times neighbor + extra).
\ Decimal strings keep even very large intermediate results exact.
To calculate a stone formula:
  Put the stone formula's base into the stone formula's result.
  Multiply the stone formula's base by the stone formula's result.
  Put the stone formula's neighbor into the stonescale.
  Convert the stone formula's factor to the stonefactortext.
  Put the stonefactortext into the stonefactor.
  Multiply the stonescale by the stonefactor.
  Add the stonescale to the stone formula's result.
  Add the stone formula's extra to the stone formula's result.
  Keep the stone formula's result for the sauce.

\ Drop 1 is the seed. Advance only with a drop index from 2 through 46.
\ Save the proposed new values separately until all five are complete.
To advance the stones given a number:
  If the number is less than 2, exit.
  If the number is greater than 46, exit.
  Convert the number to the stoneindex.
  Put the current stones' wheat into the working stone formula's base.
  Put the current stones' barley into the working stone formula's neighbor.
  Put the stoneindex into the working stone formula's extra.
  Put 3 into the working stone formula's factor.
  Calculate the working stone formula.
  Put the working stone formula's result into the next stones' wheat.
  Put the current stones' barley into the working stone formula's base.
  Put the current stones' salt into the working stone formula's neighbor.
  Put the current stones' wheat into the working stone formula's extra.
  Put 5 into the working stone formula's factor.
  Calculate the working stone formula.
  Put the working stone formula's result into the next stones' barley.
  Put the current stones' salt into the working stone formula's base.
  Put the current stones' bitter into the working stone formula's neighbor.
  Put the current stones' barley into the working stone formula's extra.
  Put 7 into the working stone formula's factor.
  Calculate the working stone formula.
  Put the working stone formula's result into the next stones' salt.
  Put the current stones' bitter into the working stone formula's base.
  Put the current stones' red into the working stone formula's neighbor.
  Put the current stones' salt into the working stone formula's extra.
  Put 11 into the working stone formula's factor.
  Calculate the working stone formula.
  Put the working stone formula's result into the next stones' bitter.
  Put the current stones' red into the working stone formula's base.
  Put the current stones' wheat into the working stone formula's neighbor.
  Put the current stones' bitter into the working stone formula's extra.
  Put 13 into the working stone formula's factor.
  Calculate the working stone formula.
  Put the working stone formula's result into the next stones' red.
  Put the next stones' wheat into the current stones' wheat.
  Put the next stones' barley into the current stones' barley.
  Put the next stones' salt into the current stones' salt.
  Put the next stones' bitter into the current stones' bitter.
  Put the next stones' red into the current stones' red.

\ Each step uses one stone advancement, so CAL has no nested loops.
To reach the stones for a number:
  Begin the stone sequence.
  Put 2 into the stoneposition.
  Loop.
    If the stoneposition is greater than the number, break.
    If the stoneposition is greater than 46, break.
    Advance the stones given the stoneposition.
    Add 1 to the stoneposition.
  Repeat.
